2026-10-11|Sum of Squares of Special Elements

Question
Given a 1-indexed integer array `nums` of length `n`, return the sum of the squares of all elements `nums[i]` for which `n % i == 0`.

Approach

1. Initialize `sum = 0`.

2. Traverse the array using index `i` from `0` to `n - 1`.

3. Since C++ arrays use 0-based indexing but the problem uses 1-based indexing, the actual index is `i + 1`.

4. Check whether `(n % (i + 1)) == 0`.

5. If the condition is true, the element is special. Add its square to the sum:
   `sum += nums[i] * nums[i]`

6. Return the final sum.

7. Time complexity is O(n), because we examine every element once.

8. Space complexity is O(1), because we use only a constant amount of extra space.

Solution
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int sumOfSquares(vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = nums.size();
    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (n % (i + 1) == 0) {
            sum += nums[i] * nums[i];
        }
    }

    return sum;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;

    vector<int> nums(n);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cin >> nums[i];
    }

    cout << sumOfSquares(nums) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
